    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ab909ea5-68e5-5b2d-8444-377d530f30eb",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Simplify.\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\sin\\left(62^\\circ\\right)+\\cos\\left(62^\\circ\\right)\\right)^2\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Put $x=62^\\circ$. Expand the square and combine the squared terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sin x+\\cos x)^2 &=\\sin^2 x+2\\sin x\\cos x+\\cos^2 x \\\\ &=1+2\\sin x\\cos x \\\\ &=1+\\sin(2x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $\\sin^2 x+\\cos^2 x=1$ and $2\\sin x\\cos x=\\sin(2x)$. Multiply both terms by $\\frac{1}{2}$.\n\nSubstitute $x=62^\\circ$ and double the whole angle: $2x=124^\\circ$. The result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\sin\\left(124^\\circ\\right) .\n$$",
